<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="MIPS:BE:32:default">
<!--
    kuna returnbranch: MIPS hand-written code may load a constant code address
    into $ra and jump through it. SLEIGH marks `jr ra` as a RETURN whatever
    the register holds, so the function ended at the jump and the store it
    reaches was dropped.

        0x1000  or   t0,ra,zero      save the incoming return address
        0x1004  lui  ra,0x0
        0x1008  ori  ra,ra,0x1014    ra = the local continuation
        0x100c  jr   ra              lifted as RETURN
        0x1010  nop
        0x1014  sw   a0,0(a1)        the store the RETURN dropped
        0x1018  or   ra,t0,zero
        0x101c  jr   ra              the real return
        0x1020  nop

    On, a RETURN whose target the straight-line entry prefix proves constant
    flows as an indirect branch, which jump recovery follows to 0x1014. The
    second `jr ra` follows a branch, so it stays a RETURN.

    Stage: P2 lift and flow (flow classification). Two passes: option
    returnbranch off (the bug), then on (the default).

    Assert #1: off: the store is missing.
    Assert #2: on: the store is kept.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1000" readonly="true">03e040253c1f000037ff101403e0000800000000aca400000100f82503e0000800000000</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000" name="bounce"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
<com>parse line extern void bounce(int value, int *slot);</com>
<com>option returnbranch off</com>
<com>lo fu bounce</com><com>decompile</com><com>print C</com>
<com>option returnbranch on</com>
<com>lo fu bounce</com><com>decompile</com><com>print C</com>
<com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="returnbranch #1 #2: only the default keeps the store" min="1" max="1">\*slot = value;</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
